Organizers: Faizee Ali Khan (Research Assistant, NCF), Ashwin Vishwanathan (Scientist, NCF), Tarun Menon (PhD candidate, IISc)
 
Workshop Title: Using eBird Data for Ecology and Conservation
Q: Short Description of the workshop (250 words):
eBird, is now one of the world’s largest citizen science platforms. The spatiotemporal coverage offered by eBird has enabled the quantification of ecological patterns and hypotheses that explain them for entire bird communities and mountain ranges. eBird data has been used in a vast array of ecological applications including ecotype-occupancy, migrational movements, tracking bird population, and highlighting areas important for conservation and more. This workshop introduces participants to the full potential of eBird data for ecological research and conservation planning.
An overview of eBird and the kinds of data it gathers will be given at the start of the session. After that, participants will learn how to use this data to ask insightful ecological questions about long-term trends, seasonality, or species distributions. They will be guided through data extraction, cleaning, and visualization through a practical walkthrough. Understanding observer and spatial biases that can affect analyses and how to mitigate them with best practices will be emphasized. The workshop will demonstrate how to make valid deductions while recognizing limitations using case studies from a local context. Additionally, it will concentrate on how to enhance the robustness of eBird data by integrating it with other ecological datasets.
This workshop is especially valuable for students and conservation practitioners seeking to work with open-access biodiversity data. By the end, participants will have the conceptual and practical skills to responsibly use eBird data to support their own ecological research or conservation goals.
